# FORMAT — Code Academy, Document Standards & Scripting Specification

File: openOODA/FORMAT.oot
Canonical formatting standard for code academy headers, process documents, and test harnesses.
Every source file in the repository must read as a clear narrative structure.
All human text follows ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English (RULES.oot 1.10).

## 0. Repository File Laws

The repository enforces strict zero-tolerance file rules (RULES.oot & START.oot):
  1. `.oo` ONLY  — All product code, compiler, standard library, CLI, tools, and test scripts.
  2. `.oot` ONLY — All documentation, process boards, RFCs, and specifications.

Forbidden extensions:
  - No `.py` — Python is unauthorized across all workspace directories.
  - No `.sh` — except `../ooda/bootstrap/oodac_pure_build` (START.oot / RULES 1.13).
  - No `.oos` — Scripts use the same `.oo` ending as the compiler.
  - No `.md`  — All documentation must use `.oot`.

Line limit: Every `.oo` and `.oot` file must not exceed 256 lines. Comments count.
Generated `.oot` files are bounded by 256 and carry `THIS FILE IS GENERATED — DO NOT EDIT`.
Constraints breed craft. Filth breeds at infinity.

---

## 1. Part 1: .oo Code Academy Format

Every product `.oo` file (compiler, stdlib, CLI, scripts) begins with an Academy header.

### 1.1 Header Structure

// # <Title>
//
// Logline: <One sentence in present tense, active voice, ending with a period.>
//
// Setup: <Preconditions, capabilities required, or runtime assumptions.>
//
// Beats:
//   1. <First action or responsibility>
//   2. <Second action or responsibility>
//   3. <Third action or responsibility>
//
// residual: <Optional: features wanted but not yet built>

### 1.2 ASD-STE100 Header Rules
- Title: Identifies the job of the file, not its file name.
- Logline: Exactly one honest sentence in ASD-STE100. Present tense. Active voice. End with a period.
- Setup: Declares what must be true before execution (required capabilities, environment).
- Beats: Numbered list of 3 to 6 logical beats describing what the file does. Never reference line numbers.
- Residual: Optional single line immediately following Beats for unbuilt features.

### 1.3 Readability & Philosophy
- Code is told twice: Once to the machine (zero forgiveness, perfect memory), once to the human (zero memory, generous patience). The Academy header is the second telling.
- Intent is the first deliverable: Function and variable names must reveal intent. If you cannot explain it in one sentence, you do not understand it yet.
- Keep real functions: Compiler (`oodac/*.oo`) and stdlib (`std/*.oo`) files retain idiomatic functions (`typecheck_*`, `c_emit_*`, `lex_*`, `opt_*`, `res_*`). Do not insert script-level `when`/`then` constructs into compiler modules.
- Comments are decay, code is growth: Prefer rewriting code so comments become unnecessary.
- 256-line constraint: Comments count toward the 256-line limit. Split files only along natural domain seams.

### 1.4 Code Quality Loop
- Run `./oodac/oodac check <file.oo>` after edits.
- Green: proceed to the next file.
- Red: fix immediately before starting another file.

---

## 2. Part 2: .oot Document Format

All architecture boards, RFCs, documentation, and process notes use the `.oot` format.

### 2.1 Document Header Structure
Every `.oot` file begins with a standardized top block:

# <NAME> — <Domain or Purpose Title>

File: openOODA/<NAME>.oot
<Status / Context / Relationship Marker>

### 2.2 Section Structure & Formatting
- Title & Markers: H1 heading with board name and domain, followed by file path and context marker.
- Numbered & Topic Headings: Use `## 1.`, `### 1.1` or topic headers to maintain visual hierarchy.
- Language (ASD-STE100, RULES.oot 1.10): Required for all boards, RFCs, guides, and handoffs.
  Use short sentences. Put one thought in each sentence. Use simple words.
  Use the active voice. Do not use slang, jokes, or metaphors.
  Explain a technical term the first time you use it.
- Status Markers: Use standardized status vocabulary:
  - `done` — Works, verified with proof.
  - `smoke` — Small verification check passes.
  - `partial` — Partially implemented.
  - `residual` — Wanted, planned, not built yet.
  - `open` — In progress during the active sprint.
- Tables & Code Blocks: Use Markdown tables for structured mappings and fenced blocks for code snippets.
- Line limit: Strictly <= 256 lines per `.oot` document. Split oversized documents into companion boards or `rfcs/`.

### 2.3 Generational Semantic Versioning Specification
All process boards, package manifests, and release tags follow Generational Semantic Versioning:
- Format: `v<Floor>.<Thrust>.<Patch>` (Active Ecosystem Release Pin: `v2.5.0`).
- Floor (F): Generational foundation tier substrate (Gen 1-4). Increments on breaking changes to core memory, capability ABI, or bootstrap compiler substrate.
- Thrust (T): Boyd E-M performance improvements, compiler optimization passes, and non-breaking stdlib feature expansions.
- Patch (P): Adversarial security remediation, hostile test certifications, and zero-defect maintenance.
- Power Law 80/20 Weighted Floor Rule: 80% of systemic capability is anchored in the 20% core foundational primitives (linear arenas with O(1) bulk resets, 14 capability tokens, direct ELF64/WASM emission, SSA mem2reg). The Floor increments if and only if alterations affect this 20% core substrate or cross a generational tier boundary.

---

## 3. Part 3: .oo Script, Smoke Test & Adversarial Verification Conventions

All test scripts, proof harnesses, and maintenance tools live under `ooda/scripts/` as pure `.oo` files.

### 3.1 Five-Part File Structure
Every `.oo` script follows five parts in order:
1. Title block (Academy header, lines 1–15).
2. Imports (minimal, e.g. `import "std/scenario.oo";`, `import "std/sh.oo";`).
3. Constants and test fixtures.
4. `pub fn main(...)` — The test scenario expressed as executable narrative code.
5. (Optional) private helper functions (snake_case verbs).

### 3.2 Capability Injection
Scripts execute within the capability security model (SECURITY.oot). The runtime injects tokens directly into `main`:
- `pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap)` — For executing subprocesses and commands.
- `pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap, fs: &FsReadCap)` — For reading files and directories.
- `pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap, fs: &FsReadCap, fsW: &FsWriteCap)` — For workspace mutation and cleanup.
- `pub fn main(p: &ProcessCap, fs: &FsReadCap, env: &EnvCap)` — For inspecting environment variables.
- `pub fn main(alloc: &AllocCap)` — For linear arena allocations and bulk memory management.
- `pub fn main(net: &NetCap, tcp: &TcpCap)` — For network communications and socket tests.
- `pub fn main(t: &TimeCap, r: &RandCap)` — For monotonic time checks and PRNG testing.
- `pub fn main(th: &ThreadCap)` — For concurrency, mutex locks, and thread dispatch tests.

Rules:
- Never mint capabilities inside a script. Rely strictly on runtime injection.
- Request only the minimum capability tokens required (least privilege).

### 3.3 Adversarial Validation & Negative Trust Probing (Pillar 3)
- Hostile Boundary Probing: Test suites must actively probe invalid inputs, corrupted tokens, and boundary overflows.
- Negative Trust Verification: Test fail-closed paths with mutated tags, forged capability tokens, and unauthorized syscalls.
- Sequential Double-Run Execution Law:
  Always execute test targets twice sequentially in fresh process instances (`./bin/ooda run <test.oo>`).
  This proves deterministic memory safety, absence of uninitialized memory leaks, and zero ambient state contamination.

### 3.4 Test Scenario Vocabulary
Scripts use the assertion vocabulary provided by `std/scenario.oo`:
- `scenario(p, fs, name)` — Opens a named test story and warms the runner.
- `check(p, cmd, within)` — Executes a command with timeout and fails closed on error.
- File Assertions:
  - `expect_present(fs, path)` — Asserts file exists.
  - `expect_absent(fs, path)` — Asserts file does not exist.
  - `expect_line_count(fs, path, n)` — Asserts exact line count.
  - `expect_substring(fs, path, needle)` — Asserts presence of substring in file.
  - `expect_count(fs, dir, pattern, n)` — Asserts matching file count in directory.
  - `expect_no_phantoms(fs, dir, allow)` — Asserts no unexpected `.oo` files in directory.
- Process & Value Assertions:
  - `expect_clean(p, cmd)` — Asserts command exits with status 0.
  - `expect_quiet(p, cmd)` — Asserts command produces empty stdout.
  - `expect_equal(a, b)` — Asserts string equality.
  - `expect_lt(a, b)` — Asserts integer less-than relationship.

### 3.5 Script Anti-Patterns (Strictly Forbidden)
- DO NOT create `.oos` or `.sh` scripts. All scripts are pure `.oo`.
- DO NOT write `if r.is_err() { fail = 1; }` chains in `main`; use `expect_*` helpers.
- DO NOT invoke raw `process_exec` outside `main` when higher-level helpers (`sh_*`) exist.
- DO NOT use abstract variable names (`x`, `tmp`, `data`, `res`); use specific narrative names.
- DO NOT print "Test 1:", "Test 2:"; use descriptive scenario names.
- DO NOT write test scripts exceeding 256 lines; split complex harnesses into distinct smoke files.

### 3.6 Automated Verification
Run `ooda/scripts/script_lint.oo` and `openOODA/scripts/enforcer.oo` before committing:
- Validates Academy headers (Title, Logline, Setup, Beats).
- Enforces zero forbidden files (`.py`, `.sh`, `.md`, `.js`).
- Checks that `process_exec` is not called outside `main`.
- Enforces strict <= 256 line limit on all `.oo` and `.oot` files.
